export const W6={title:'Diversity and disparities: sex and gender, disability, sexual and gender minorities, language and intersectionality',hook:'A deaf patient receives discharge instructions only in writing above her reading level. A transgender teenager avoids a clinic because forms list only two gender options. A woman’s heart attack is mistaken for anxiety. Disparities extend across many dimensions of identity, and people often belong to several groups at once.',objectives:['Identify populations NIMHD designates as experiencing health disparities.','Describe disparities related to sex and gender, disability, sexual and gender minority status, language and immigration.','Explain intersectionality and minority stress.','Propose inclusive practices that reduce these disparities.'],sections:[
['Who experiences health disparities',[
'The National Institute on Minority Health and Health Disparities (NIMHD) identifies populations that experience health disparities in the United States: racial and ethnic minority groups, people with lower socioeconomic status, underserved rural communities, sexual and gender minority groups and, since 2023, people with disabilities. Disparities for these groups appear in rates of disease, access to care, quality of care and outcomes. KFF similarly notes that disparities occur across race and ethnicity, socioeconomic status, geography, language, gender, disability, citizenship and sexual orientation.',
'These categories are not separate boxes. A person can be rural, low-income, disabled and a member of a racial minority group at the same time. **Intersectionality**, a term introduced by legal scholar Kimberlé Crenshaw in 1989, describes how overlapping identities create distinct experiences of advantage and disadvantage that cannot be understood by examining one identity alone.',
flip('NIMHD-designated populations',[['Racial and ethnic minority groups','Including Black, Hispanic, Asian, AIAN and NHPI populations'],['Lower socioeconomic status','Lower income, education or occupational status'],['Underserved rural communities','Distance, workforce and infrastructure barriers'],['Sexual and gender minority groups','LGBTQ+ people'],['People with disabilities','Designated as a health disparity population in 2023'],['Intersectionality','Overlapping identities producing distinct experiences']])]],
['Sex and gender',[
'Biological sex and gender (social roles and identity) both influence health. In the United States, men have shorter life expectancy than women, partly due to higher rates of injury, substance use, suicide and delayed care-seeking. Women experience disparities in research and diagnosis: for decades, many clinical trials included few women, and the NIH Revitalization Act of 1993 required inclusion of women and minorities in NIH-funded clinical research. Heart attack symptoms in women can include shortness of breath, nausea or back or jaw pain in addition to chest pain, and research has found women are less likely to receive some guideline-recommended cardiac care. Maternal health disparities, covered earlier, affect women of color most severely.']],
['Disability',[
'People with disabilities make up a large share of the population and face barriers that are often physical, communicative and attitudinal rather than medical. Examples include examination tables and imaging machines that cannot accommodate wheelchair users, lack of sign-language interpreters, written materials not available in accessible formats, and assumptions by clinicians about quality of life. The Americans with Disabilities Act and Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act require health programs to provide reasonable accommodations and effective communication. **Diagnostic overshadowing**—attributing new symptoms to an existing disability—can delay diagnosis.',
sort('Barrier type for patients with disabilities',['Physical barrier','Communication barrier','Attitudinal barrier'],[['No height-adjustable exam table',0],['Scale that cannot weigh a wheelchair user',0],['No sign-language interpreter available',1],['Instructions only in small print',1],['Assuming a disabled patient does not want preventive screening',2],['Speaking to a companion instead of the patient',2]])]],
['Sexual and gender minorities',[
'Lesbian, gay, bisexual, transgender, queer and other sexual and gender minority (SGM) people experience higher rates of some mental health conditions, substance use and certain chronic conditions. The **minority stress model** explains part of this pattern: chronic stress from stigma, discrimination, concealment and rejection affects health. SGM people also report discrimination in health care and may delay care. Many health systems now collect **sexual orientation and gender identity (SOGI)** data voluntarily and confidentially so disparities can be measured, and use inclusive forms, correct names and pronouns, and clinician training to create welcoming environments.']],
['Language, literacy and immigration',[
'People with **limited English proficiency** face risks of miscommunication, medication errors and lower satisfaction. Federal civil rights law requires meaningful language access in federally funded programs, including qualified interpreters. **Health literacy**—the ability to find, understand and use health information—affects people of every background; plain language, teach-back and visual aids help. Immigrants, especially those without citizenship, face eligibility limits for public coverage, fear of immigration consequences and language barriers; KFF reports that noncitizens had an uninsured rate of about 31% in 2024 compared with 8.0% for citizens.',
kase('Inclusive care at a clinic visit',{a:{text:'A deaf patient who uses American Sign Language arrives for a visit about a new diabetes diagnosis. Staff suggest writing notes back and forth. What is best?',choices:[['Provide a qualified sign-language interpreter (in person or video)','b','Correct. Effective communication is required, and complex medical information needs an interpreter.',true],['Use written notes for the whole visit','b','Notes may not provide effective communication for complex information, especially if English is not the patient’s first language.',false]]},b:{text:'The patient also lives in a rural area and has limited transportation. What helps her follow-up care?',choices:[['Offer video visits with interpreter support and coordinate transportation resources','c','Correct. Intersecting barriers need combined solutions.',true],['Schedule weekly in-person visits with no support','c','This ignores her transportation barrier.',false]]},c:{text:'The patient receives education through an interpreter, uses teach-back to confirm understanding and enrolls in video follow-up. Her care plan addresses language, distance and disability together.',end:true}})]],
['Other groups facing disparities',[
"Beyond NIMHD’s designated populations, other groups experience significant disparities. **Older adults** may face ageism, financial barriers, isolation and difficulty accessing care, especially in rural areas. **People experiencing homelessness** have far higher rates of chronic disease, mental illness, substance use and early death, and find it hard to store medications, keep appointments or recover after hospital discharge. **People who are incarcerated or recently released** experience high rates of infectious disease, mental illness and substance use, with a sharply elevated risk of overdose death shortly after release. **Veterans** may face service-related conditions such as traumatic brain injury and post-traumatic stress, and rural veterans may live far from Veterans Affairs facilities. **Migrant and seasonal farmworkers** face occupational hazards, heat exposure, crowded housing and limited access to care.",
"Recognizing these groups matters for planning: a clinic that serves people experiencing homelessness may need walk-in hours, medication storage solutions and partnerships with shelters; programs for people leaving incarceration may provide naloxone and connections to treatment at release. As with every group, people’s needs vary, and the best programs involve affected people in design. In essays, naming specific groups and specific barriers, rather than speaking about “vulnerable populations” in general, shows deeper understanding.",
{t:'table',caption:"Group-specific barriers and responses",head:["Group","Common barrier","Example response"],rows:[["People experiencing homelessness","No place to store medications or recover","Medical respite care and shelter partnerships"],["People leaving incarceration","High overdose risk after release","Naloxone and treatment linkage at release"],["Rural veterans","Distance to VA facilities","Telehealth and community care networks"],["Farmworkers","Heat exposure, limited care access","Mobile clinics and heat-safety rules"]]}]]
],takeaways:['NIMHD disparity populations: racial and ethnic minorities, lower SES, underserved rural, sexual and gender minorities and people with disabilities.','Intersectionality: overlapping identities create distinct experiences; minority stress affects SGM health.','Accessible equipment, interpreters, plain language and inclusive data collection reduce disparities.'],terms:[['Intersectionality','Overlapping social identities creating distinct experiences of advantage and disadvantage.'],['Minority stress','Chronic stress from stigma and discrimination that affects health.'],['Health literacy','Ability to find, understand and use health information.']],mcqs:[q('Which group did NIMHD designate as a health disparity population in 2023?','People with disabilities','Older adults','Athletes','Health care workers','People with disabilities were added in 2023.'),q('Intersectionality describes:','How overlapping identities create distinct experiences','A type of medical imaging','A single cause of disease','Equal treatment for all','Crenshaw introduced the term in 1989.'),q('The minority stress model explains:','How stigma and discrimination contribute to health disparities','Why stress is always beneficial','Genetic differences','Insurance rules','Chronic stress affects health.'),q('Which law required inclusion of women and minorities in NIH-funded clinical research?','NIH Revitalization Act of 1993','HIPAA','EMTALA','The Affordable Care Act','It addressed underrepresentation in research.'),q('An attitudinal barrier for a patient with a disability is:','Assuming the patient does not want preventive screening','A narrow doorway','No interpreter','Small print','Attitudes can limit care.'),q('According to KFF, noncitizens in 2024 had an uninsured rate of about:','31%','8%','2%','60%','Citizens had about 8.0%.'),q('Health literacy is best supported by:','Plain language, teach-back and visual aids','More medical jargon','Longer written forms','Speaking faster','These help understanding.'),q('Collecting SOGI data helps health systems:','Measure and address disparities for sexual and gender minorities','Deny care','Reduce privacy','Replace clinical exams','Data make disparities visible.')],frq:{prompt:'A rural clinic serves patients who include people with disabilities, people with limited English proficiency and LGBTQ+ youth. Explain the disparities each group may face, apply the concept of intersectionality, and propose three inclusive practices.',rubric:[['Disparities for each group',['disability','limited english','lgbtq','sexual and gender','barrier']],['Apply intersectionality',['intersectional','overlap','multiple','rural']],['Three inclusive practices',['interpreter','accessible','sogi','inclusive','teach-back','training']],['Link to sources or populations',['nimhd','minority stress','ada']]],model:'People with disabilities may face inaccessible equipment, communication barriers and assumptions about their needs; people with limited English proficiency face miscommunication and lower-quality care without qualified interpreters; and LGBTQ+ youth may face stigma, minority stress and avoidance of care. NIMHD recognizes underserved rural communities, sexual and gender minorities and people with disabilities as health disparity populations. Intersectionality means a patient who is, for example, rural, disabled and has limited English faces combined barriers that require combined solutions. The clinic could provide qualified interpreters and accessible equipment and materials, collect voluntary sexual orientation and gender identity data with inclusive forms and pronouns, train staff on bias and use teach-back, and offer telehealth to reduce distance barriers.'},sources:[RULES,NIMHD,KFF_UNINS]};

export const W7={title:'Bias, communication and disparities in treatment',hook:'In 2003, a landmark national report concluded that racial and ethnic minority patients received lower-quality care than White patients even when insurance and income were similar. Two decades later, research still finds differences in pain treatment, communication and referrals. Disparities in treatment happen inside clinics and hospitals, which means clinicians and health systems can change them.',objectives:['Summarize the findings of the Institute of Medicine’s Unequal Treatment report.','Explain explicit and implicit bias and how they can affect clinical decisions.','Describe disparities in pain treatment and communication.','Explain trust, shared decision-making and patient-clinician concordance.'],sections:[
['Unequal Treatment',[
'In 2003, the Institute of Medicine (now the National Academy of Medicine) published *Unequal Treatment: Confronting Racial and Ethnic Disparities in Health Care*. It found that racial and ethnic minorities tended to receive lower-quality health care than White patients across a range of conditions and procedures, even when factors such as insurance status and income were comparable. The report identified contributors at three levels: health system factors (such as fragmented care and financial incentives), patient-level factors (such as preferences and mistrust) and the clinical encounter, including clinician bias, stereotyping and uncertainty. It recommended increasing awareness, standardizing care through evidence-based guidelines, improving interpreter services, increasing workforce diversity and collecting data on race and ethnicity.',
match('Match each factor to its level in Unequal Treatment',[['Fragmented care and financial incentives','Health system level'],['Mistrust based on past experiences','Patient level'],['Clinician stereotyping during a visit','Clinical encounter level'],['Lack of interpreters','Health system level'],['Clinical uncertainty filled by assumptions','Clinical encounter level']])]],
['Explicit and implicit bias',[
'**Explicit bias** consists of conscious beliefs and attitudes. **Implicit bias** consists of automatic associations that operate without awareness and can conflict with a person’s stated values. Implicit biases develop from culture and experience and are found across professions. Under time pressure, fatigue and uncertainty—common in health care—people rely more on automatic judgments. Bias can shape how symptoms are interpreted, how much time is spent explaining options and which tests or referrals are offered.',
'Strategies to reduce the effect of bias include standardized protocols and checklists, structured decision tools, reflection and training, monitoring outcomes by group to detect differences, and creating time for patient-centered communication. Training alone has limited lasting effect; system changes that make equitable care the default are more reliable.',
sort('Explicit bias, implicit bias or system safeguard?',['Explicit bias','Implicit bias','System safeguard'],[['A clinician openly states a group exaggerates pain',0],['Automatically rating one patient’s pain lower without awareness',1],['Spending less time explaining options to some patients without noticing',1],['Standardized pain assessment protocol for every patient',2],['Tracking analgesia rates by race to detect gaps',2]])]],
['Pain treatment and communication',[
'Studies have repeatedly found racial and ethnic differences in pain treatment, including less frequent analgesia for Black and Hispanic patients with similar injuries in some emergency department studies. A 2016 study published in *Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences* found that a substantial share of White medical students and residents endorsed false beliefs about biological differences between Black and White people—such as Black people having thicker skin—and that those who endorsed such beliefs rated a Black patient’s pain as lower. Communication differences also appear: research has found shorter visits, less patient-centered communication and less involvement in decisions for some minority patients.',
bench('Is the claim supported?',[{text:'Unequal Treatment (2003) found that minority patients received lower-quality care even when insurance and income were comparable.',claim:'According to the report, differences in insurance alone fully explained disparities in care.',supported:false,why:'The report found disparities persisted even with comparable insurance and income.'},{text:'A 2016 study found that clinicians-in-training who endorsed false beliefs about biological differences rated a Black patient’s pain lower.',claim:'False beliefs about biology were associated with lower pain ratings in that study.',supported:true,why:'This matches the reported association.'}])]],
['Trust, history and shared decisions',[
'Trust affects whether people seek care, share information and follow recommendations. Mistrust among some communities is rooted in real history—the U.S. Public Health Service syphilis study at Tuskegee, nonconsensual sterilizations and the use of Henrietta Lacks’s cells without consent—as well as contemporary experiences of discrimination. Trust is rebuilt through respect, transparency, consistency and community partnership, not by asking patients to set aside their experiences.',
'**Shared decision-making** involves clinicians and patients deciding together, combining medical evidence with the patient’s values and preferences. **Patient-clinician concordance**—shared race, ethnicity, language or gender—has been associated in some studies with better communication and satisfaction, which is one reason workforce diversity is a recommended strategy. All clinicians, regardless of concordance, can practice cultural humility, use interpreters, check understanding and invite questions.',
order('Steps in shared decision-making',['Explain that a decision needs to be made and that the patient’s view matters','Describe the options with benefits and risks in plain language','Ask about the patient’s values, goals and concerns','Check understanding with teach-back','Decide together or agree on a time to decide','Document the decision and plan follow-up'])]],
['Apply it to a clinical case',[
kase('Pain assessment in the emergency department',{a:{text:'Two patients arrive with similar long-bone fractures. One is White, one is Black. Both rate pain 8 of 10. Which approach best prevents biased treatment?',choices:[['Apply the same standardized pain protocol to both','b','Correct. Protocols reduce the role of automatic judgments.',true],['Rely on each nurse’s impression of how much pain each patient seems to have','b','Unstructured impressions are where implicit bias can enter.',false]]},b:{text:'A quality review finds that over six months Black patients with fractures received analgesia less often. What should the department do?',choices:[['Investigate causes, retrain staff, reinforce protocols and keep monitoring rates by group','c','Correct. Measurement plus system change addresses the gap.',true],['Conclude the data are wrong and take no action','c','Ignoring data allows disparities to persist.',false]]},c:{text:'After protocol reinforcement and feedback, analgesia rates converge. The department continues to report rates by race and ethnicity each quarter.',end:true}})]],
['Workforce diversity and the CLAS Standards',[
"A diverse health workforce is one of the most consistently recommended strategies for reducing disparities. Clinicians from underrepresented backgrounds are more likely to practice in underserved communities, and workforce diversity can improve communication, trust and the relevance of research. Pipeline programs—such as health science courses, mentoring and organizations like HOSA—help students from all backgrounds enter health careers. Diversity in leadership and research teams also shapes which questions are asked and which problems are prioritized.",
"The **National Standards for Culturally and Linguistically Appropriate Services (CLAS)**, published by the HHS Office of Minority Health, give organizations a blueprint for equitable care. The principal standard calls for effective, equitable, understandable and respectful quality care and services that are responsive to diverse cultural health beliefs and practices, preferred languages, health literacy and other communication needs. Other standards address governance and leadership, workforce training, language assistance at no cost, plain-language materials, community engagement, data collection and continuous improvement. Accreditation bodies and payers increasingly expect organizations to measure and act on disparities; for example, The Joint Commission introduced health care equity requirements for accredited hospitals in 2023.",
"Cultural competence is sometimes described as a set of skills for working across cultures, while cultural humility emphasizes lifelong self-reflection and partnership. In practice, organizations combine both: training in communication and interpreter use, policies that make language access routine, and feedback from patients and communities about whether care feels respectful. These system supports make it easier for every clinician to deliver equitable care."]]
],takeaways:['Unequal Treatment (2003): minorities received lower-quality care even with comparable insurance and income.','Implicit bias operates automatically; standardized protocols and monitoring reduce its effects.','Trust, shared decision-making, interpreters and workforce diversity improve equitable care.'],terms:[['Implicit bias','Automatic associations that affect judgments without awareness.'],['Shared decision-making','Clinicians and patients deciding together using evidence and patient values.'],['Concordance','Shared characteristics such as race or language between patient and clinician.']],mcqs:[q('The 2003 report Unequal Treatment concluded that minority patients:','Received lower-quality care even when insurance and income were comparable','Received better care than White patients','Had disparities only because of insurance','Were never affected by bias','Disparities persisted after accounting for these factors.'),q('Implicit bias is:','Automatic associations that operate without awareness','A conscious, openly stated belief','A legal requirement','A type of insurance','It can conflict with stated values.'),q('Which is a system safeguard against biased pain treatment?','A standardized pain assessment protocol','Relying on impressions','Shorter visits','Fewer interpreters','Protocols reduce discretion where bias enters.'),q('A 2016 PNAS study found that trainees who endorsed false beliefs about biological differences:','Rated a Black patient’s pain as lower','Prescribed more pain medication','Had no different ratings','Refused to treat patients','The beliefs were associated with lower pain ratings.'),q('Which historical event contributes to mistrust of medical research in some communities?','The Tuskegee syphilis study','The Alma-Ata Declaration','The ACA','Healthy People 2030','Tuskegee withheld treatment without consent.'),q('Shared decision-making combines:','Medical evidence with the patient’s values and preferences','The clinician’s preferences only','Insurance rules only','Family decisions without the patient','Decisions are made together.'),q('Patient-clinician concordance has been associated with:','Better communication and satisfaction in some studies','Worse outcomes in all cases','Lower costs only','Elimination of all disparities','This supports workforce diversity.'),q('Unequal Treatment identified contributors at which levels?','Health system, patient and clinical encounter','Only the patient','Only the government','Only insurance companies','Three levels interact.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how disparities can arise during the clinical encounter, using findings from Unequal Treatment and research on pain treatment, and propose system-level strategies to reduce them.',rubric:[['Unequal Treatment findings',['unequal treatment','2003','lower-quality','insurance','comparable']],['Bias and pain disparities',['implicit','bias','pain','false belief','analgesia']],['Trust and communication',['trust','communication','shared decision','interpreter']],['System strategies',['protocol','standard','monitor','workforce','data','training']]],model:'The 2003 Institute of Medicine report Unequal Treatment found that racial and ethnic minority patients received lower-quality care than White patients even when insurance and income were comparable, with contributors at the system, patient and clinical encounter levels, including clinician bias and uncertainty. Research on pain treatment has found less frequent analgesia for some minority patients, and a 2016 study linked false beliefs about biological differences to lower pain ratings for a Black patient. Mistrust rooted in history and differences in communication can reduce shared decision-making. Strategies include standardized protocols such as pain assessment pathways, monitoring quality measures by race and ethnicity, qualified interpreters, bias training paired with system changes, shared decision-making and a more diverse workforce.'},sources:[RULES,NAM_UNEQUAL,KFF_DISP]};

export const W8={title:'Research, data and clinical tools: where disparities hide',hook:'For years, a widely used kidney function equation adjusted results based on whether a patient was Black, which could delay referrals for kidney transplants. Pulse oximeters can overestimate oxygen levels in people with darker skin. Research, data and the tools built from them can carry disparities unless they are designed and tested with everyone in mind.',objectives:['Explain the NIMHD Research Framework’s domains and levels of influence.','Describe underrepresentation in clinical research and efforts to address it.','Explain how race-based clinical algorithms and device limitations can create disparities.','Evaluate data and tools for equity.'],sections:[
['The NIMHD Research Framework',[
'NIMHD’s Research Framework organizes the factors that influence minority health and health disparities in a grid. Its **five domains of influence** are **biological**, **behavioral**, **physical/built environment**, **sociocultural environment** and **health care system**. Its **four levels of influence** are **individual**, **interpersonal**, **community** and **societal**. Each cell contains example factors; for instance, genetic risk sits in the biological domain at the individual level, community resources sit in the health care system domain at the community level, and societal structural discrimination sits in the sociocultural domain at the societal level. The framework combines a socioecological model with an earlier health disparities research framework and helps researchers see that a disparity can have causes in many cells at once.',
match('Place each factor in the NIMHD grid',[['Genetic risk for a disease','Biological domain · Individual level'],['Family exercise habits','Behavioral domain · Interpersonal level'],['Neighborhood air pollution','Physical/built environment · Community level'],['Societal structural discrimination','Sociocultural environment · Societal level'],['Availability of community clinics','Health care system · Community level'],['National health insurance policy','Health care system · Societal level']])]],
['Who is included in research',[
'When clinical trials include few participants from some groups, results may not apply equally to everyone, and differences in side effects or effectiveness can go undetected. Women, older adults, racial and ethnic minority groups and people with disabilities have historically been underrepresented. The NIH Revitalization Act of 1993 required inclusion of women and minorities in NIH-funded clinical research, and a 2022 federal law directed the Food and Drug Administration to require diversity action plans for certain late-stage clinical trials. Barriers to participation include mistrust, eligibility criteria that exclude people with common conditions, study sites far from communities, time and cost burdens and lack of information in patients’ languages. **Community-based participatory research** partners with communities in designing and conducting studies, which can build trust and relevance.',
sort('Barrier to research participation or strategy to improve it?',['Barrier','Strategy'],[['Study sites only in distant academic centers',0],['Consent forms only in English',0],['Strict exclusion of people with common chronic conditions',0],['Community advisory boards shaping study design',1],['Reimbursing travel and time costs',1],['Diversity action plans for late-stage trials',1]])]],
['Race-based algorithms and biased tools',[
'Some clinical calculators historically included race as a variable. The **estimated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR)** equation for kidney function used a race coefficient that produced higher estimated kidney function for Black patients, which could delay diagnosis, specialist referral and placement on the kidney transplant waiting list. In 2021, the National Kidney Foundation and American Society of Nephrology recommended a new race-free equation, and the national transplant network later required race-neutral eGFR calculations for waiting-time purposes. Other tools, such as a vaginal birth after cesarean calculator, have also been revised to remove race. The lesson is that race is a social category; using it as a biological shortcut can embed past inequities into future care.',
'Devices can also perform differently across groups. The FDA has warned that **pulse oximeters** may be less accurate in people with darker skin pigmentation, potentially overestimating oxygen levels and delaying treatment. **Artificial intelligence** tools trained on unrepresentative data or on past spending patterns can reproduce disparities; for example, an algorithm that predicts health needs from past costs may underestimate needs of groups who historically had less access to care.',
bench('Is the claim supported?',[{text:'In 2021, the National Kidney Foundation and American Society of Nephrology recommended a race-free equation for estimating kidney function.',claim:'Kidney specialty organizations recommended removing race from the eGFR calculation.',supported:true,why:'This accurately restates the 2021 recommendation.'},{text:'The FDA has warned that pulse oximeters may be less accurate in people with darker skin.',claim:'Pulse oximeters are perfectly accurate for every patient.',supported:false,why:'The FDA identified accuracy limitations related to skin pigmentation.'}])]],
['Data for equity',[
'Equity requires data that are complete, accurate and disaggregated. Health systems collect race, ethnicity, language, disability and sexual orientation and gender identity data, ideally by self-report, to measure quality by group. When data are missing or lumped into broad categories, disparities become invisible. Equity dashboards stratify measures—such as blood pressure control or wait times—by group so leaders can see gaps and track progress. Privacy protections and clear explanations of why data are collected help patients trust the process.',
order('Evaluate a clinical tool for equity',['Identify the variables the tool uses, including any race or ethnicity variable','Check whose data trained or validated the tool','Compare the tool’s accuracy across groups','Ask whether a variable is a proxy for access or bias rather than biology','Revise or replace the tool and communicate the change','Monitor outcomes by group after implementation'])]],
['Putting the framework to work',[
'Use the NIMHD grid to analyze any disparity. For example, the higher prevalence of uncontrolled hypertension in some communities may involve biological factors (individual), family diet and stress (interpersonal), food access and neighborhood safety (community), clinic availability (health care system, community) and structural discrimination and policy (societal). Solutions can target several cells at once. On the test, expect questions about the framework’s domains and levels and about examples of biased tools or underrepresentation in research.']],
['Measuring equity inside health systems',[
"Health systems can measure equity the same way they measure quality: by stratifying standard measures by race, ethnicity, language, income, disability, geography and other characteristics. Examples include rates of blood pressure control, diabetes control, cancer screening, timely prenatal care, readmissions, pain treatment and patient experience. When a measure shows a gap, teams investigate root causes and test improvements, then monitor whether the gap narrows. Federal agencies such as the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services have published health equity frameworks that encourage stratified reporting and equity-focused quality programs.",
"Collecting accurate demographic data requires care. Patients should be able to self-identify, staff need training to ask respectfully, and patients should understand that the information is used to improve care and is protected. Missing data are common and can hide gaps, so organizations track completeness. Small numbers can make some groups’ rates unstable, so combining years or using statistical methods may be necessary. These technical details matter because a disparity that is not measured is unlikely to be addressed.",
{t:'table',caption:"Equity data checklist",head:["Question","Why it matters"],rows:[["Are race, ethnicity, language and disability data self-reported?","Self-report is most accurate"],["How complete are the data?","Missing data can hide disparities"],["Are categories disaggregated?","Broad groups mask subgroup gaps"],["Are measures stratified by group?","Averages can hide differences"],["Are results shared and acted on?","Measurement must lead to improvement"]]}]]
],takeaways:['NIMHD framework: five domains (biological, behavioral, physical/built environment, sociocultural, health care system) × four levels (individual, interpersonal, community, societal).','Underrepresentation in research limits generalizability; diversity action plans and community partnership help.','Race-based eGFR was replaced in 2021; pulse oximeters may be less accurate on darker skin; evaluate tools by group.'],terms:[['NIMHD Research Framework','Grid of domains and levels of influence on health disparities.'],['Community-based participatory research','Research conducted in partnership with communities.'],['Race-based algorithm','A clinical tool that adjusts results using race.']],mcqs:[q('How many domains of influence does the NIMHD Research Framework include?','Five','Four','Three','Ten','Biological, behavioral, physical/built environment, sociocultural and health care system.'),q('Which are the NIMHD framework’s levels of influence?','Individual, interpersonal, community and societal','Local, state, federal and global','Primary, secondary and tertiary','Biological and behavioral only','There are four levels.'),q('In the NIMHD framework, genetic risk is placed in the:','Biological domain at the individual level','Societal level of the health care system','Behavioral domain at the community level','Built environment at the interpersonal level','This is NIMHD’s example.'),q('In 2021, kidney organizations recommended an eGFR equation that:','Does not use race','Uses race for all patients','Uses income','Uses height only','The race coefficient was removed.'),q('The FDA has warned that pulse oximeters may:','Be less accurate in people with darker skin','Cause infections','Work only in children','Measure blood sugar','Overestimation can delay treatment.'),q('The NIH Revitalization Act of 1993 required:','Inclusion of women and minorities in NIH-funded clinical research','Universal health coverage','Removal of race from eGFR','Medicaid expansion','It addressed research inclusion.'),q('An algorithm that predicts health needs from past spending may be biased because:','Groups with less past access spent less despite similar needs','Spending always equals need','Algorithms cannot use data','Costs are random','Past access gaps distort cost-based predictions.'),q('Community-based participatory research:','Partners with communities in designing and conducting studies','Excludes community members','Uses only animal studies','Requires no consent','Partnership builds trust and relevance.')],frq:{prompt:'Use the NIMHD Research Framework to analyze a disparity in hypertension control, and explain how research participation and clinical tools can either reduce or reinforce such disparities.',rubric:[['Framework domains and levels applied',['biological','behavioral','built environment','sociocultural','health care system','individual','community','societal']],['Research inclusion',['underrepresent','trial','revitalization','diversity action','participatory']],['Biased tools',['egfr','race','pulse oximeter','algorithm']],['Equity strategies for data and tools',['disaggregat','monitor','validate','by group']]],model:'Using the NIMHD framework, a hypertension disparity can involve biological factors at the individual level, family diet and stress at the interpersonal level, food access and neighborhood safety in the physical and built environment at the community level, clinic availability in the health care system domain and structural discrimination in the sociocultural domain at the societal level. Research can reduce disparities when trials include diverse participants, as required for NIH research since 1993 and encouraged through diversity action plans and community-based participatory research, but underrepresentation limits how well results apply. Clinical tools can reinforce disparities when they use race as a biological shortcut, as the old eGFR equation did, or when devices such as pulse oximeters perform worse on darker skin. Health systems should validate tools across groups, collect disaggregated data and monitor outcomes by group.'},sources:[RULES,NIMHD_FW,NKF_EGFR,FDA_OX]};

export const W9={title:'Policies and programs that reduce disparities',hook:'Extending health coverage, funding community clinics, raising family incomes and investing in primary care are all policies that change health outcomes for millions of people. Reducing disparities at scale requires policy, and this lesson connects U.S. and global policy tools to the evidence about what they achieve.',objectives:['Describe Healthy People 2030 goals related to health equity.','Explain how coverage programs and health centers expand access.','Describe income and social policies that affect health.','Summarize global strategies such as universal health coverage and primary health care.'],sections:[
['National goals',[
'Healthy People 2030, the U.S. national health objectives for the decade, includes among its overarching goals eliminating health disparities, achieving health equity and attaining health literacy to improve the health and well-being of all. It sets measurable objectives, tracks them by population group and places social determinants of health at the center of its framework. National goals give agencies, states and communities shared targets and data for evaluating progress.']],
['Coverage and access programs',[
'**Medicaid** covers many low-income people and is the largest source of coverage for nonelderly people with low incomes; the **Children’s Health Insurance Program (CHIP)** covers children in families with incomes too high for Medicaid. The **Affordable Care Act** expanded Medicaid (in states that chose to), created marketplaces with income-based subsidies and banned denial of coverage for preexisting conditions. KFF reports that coverage gains after the ACA narrowed, but did not eliminate, racial and ethnic gaps in insurance. Many states have extended postpartum Medicaid coverage to twelve months to address maternal health disparities.',
'**Health centers**, supported by the federal Health Resources and Services Administration (HRSA), provide primary care in underserved areas regardless of a patient’s ability to pay, with fees on a sliding scale. HRSA-funded health centers serve more than 30 million people each year. The **Indian Health Service** provides care to members of federally recognized American Indian and Alaska Native tribes. The **National Health Service Corps** supports clinicians who work in shortage areas.',
match('Match the program to its role',[['Coverage for many low-income people','Medicaid'],['Coverage for children above Medicaid limits','CHIP'],['Sliding-scale primary care in underserved areas','HRSA health centers'],['Care for members of federally recognized tribes','Indian Health Service'],['Support for clinicians in shortage areas','National Health Service Corps'],['Marketplaces, subsidies and preexisting condition protections','Affordable Care Act']])]],
['Policies beyond health care',[
'Because social determinants drive much of health, non-health policies matter. Income supports such as the Earned Income Tax Credit and child tax credits reduce poverty; KFF notes that the temporarily expanded Child Tax Credit in 2021 was associated with reduced child poverty, especially among Black and Hispanic children, and that poverty rebounded after it expired. Food assistance, housing assistance, early childhood education, minimum wage policies, paid sick leave and environmental regulations all influence health. **Health in All Policies** is an approach that asks every sector—transportation, housing, education, labor—to consider the health effects of its decisions.',
sort('Health care policy or policy beyond health care?',['Health care policy','Policy beyond health care'],[['Medicaid expansion',0],['Funding community health centers',0],['Postpartum coverage extension',0],['Expanded child tax credit',1],['Affordable housing programs',1],['Paid sick leave laws',1]])]],
['Global strategies',[
'Globally, WHO promotes **universal health coverage** (Sustainable Development Goal target 3.8) and **primary health care** as the foundation for health equity. WHO describes investing in primary health care, pooling financial resources to protect people from catastrophic costs, and monitoring health inequalities as key actions; its Health Inequality Monitoring Network helps countries measure gaps. WHO’s primary health care fact sheet projects that scaling up primary care in low- and middle-income countries could add 3.7 years to average life expectancy by 2030. KFF’s global health policy work tracks U.S. contributions such as PEPFAR (the President’s Emergency Plan for AIDS Relief, launched in 2003) and support for the Global Fund to Fight AIDS, Tuberculosis and Malaria.',
order('How a policy reduces a disparity (example: Medicaid expansion)',['Policy expands eligibility for coverage','More low-income adults enroll','Enrollees gain a usual source of care and lower out-of-pocket costs','Preventive care and chronic disease management increase','Medical debt and delayed care decrease','Outcome gaps narrow over time, measured by group'])]],
['Evaluating policy evidence',[
'Policy evaluation compares outcomes before and after a policy, or between places that adopted it and places that did not, while accounting for other differences. Medicaid expansion created a natural experiment because some states expanded and others did not; KFF reports the uninsured rate was 14.5% in non-expansion states compared with 8.0% in expansion states in 2024. Strong evaluations look at coverage, access, outcomes and equity—whether gaps between groups narrowed—not only averages. Policies can also have unintended effects, such as coverage losses when continuous Medicaid enrollment ended, which is why ongoing monitoring matters.',
bench('Is the policy claim supported?',[{text:'KFF: the temporarily expanded Child Tax Credit in 2021 was associated with reduced child poverty for Black and Hispanic children; poverty rebounded after it expired.',claim:'The expanded credit was associated with lower child poverty while it was in place.',supported:true,why:'This restates KFF’s description accurately.'},{text:'KFF: the uninsured rate was 14.5% in non-expansion states and 8.0% in expansion states in 2024.',claim:'This comparison alone proves Medicaid expansion is the only cause of the difference.',supported:false,why:'States differ in other ways; the comparison shows an association that evaluations must examine carefully.'}])]],
['Why some policies fall short',[
"Even well-designed policies can fail to reduce disparities. **Eligibility gaps** leave some people out, such as adults in states without Medicaid expansion or immigrants subject to waiting periods. **Administrative burdens**—complex applications, documentation requirements and frequent renewals—cause eligible people to lose coverage, as seen during Medicaid unwinding. **Funding limits** can mean that a program exists on paper but cannot meet demand. **Uneven implementation** across states or regions produces different results for similar people. And **unintended consequences** can arise, as when cost-sharing increases reduce use of necessary care among low-income patients.",
"Policy design for equity therefore asks who might be missed and how to reach them: automatic enrollment, simplified renewals, outreach through trusted community organizations, multilingual materials and data monitoring by group. Global programs face similar issues: financing can be fragmented across donors, and national programs may not reach remote or marginalized populations. WHO emphasizes strengthening health systems and monitoring inequalities so that national averages do not hide groups left behind.",
{t:'table',caption:"Policy barriers and design solutions",head:["Barrier","Design solution"],rows:[["Complex applications","Simplified forms and automatic enrollment"],["Frequent renewals causing coverage loss","Continuous eligibility and data-matching renewals"],["Language barriers","Multilingual outreach and navigators"],["Uneven implementation","Monitoring results by state and group"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Healthy People 2030 aims to eliminate disparities, achieve health equity and attain health literacy.','Medicaid, CHIP, the ACA, HRSA health centers, the IHS and the NHSC expand access; non-health policies such as income supports also improve health.','Globally, WHO promotes universal health coverage and primary health care; evaluate policies by whether gaps narrow.'],terms:[['Health in All Policies','Considering health effects in decisions across all sectors.'],['HRSA health center','Federally supported clinic providing sliding-scale care in underserved areas.'],['Natural experiment','A comparison created when some places adopt a policy and others do not.']],mcqs:[q('Healthy People 2030’s overarching goals include:','Eliminating health disparities and achieving health equity','Closing all hospitals','Ending Medicaid','Replacing primary care','Equity is central to Healthy People 2030.'),q('Which program covers children in families with incomes too high for Medicaid?','CHIP','Medicare','Indian Health Service','PEPFAR','CHIP fills this gap.'),q('HRSA-funded health centers provide care:','Regardless of ability to pay, with sliding-scale fees','Only to insured patients','Only to people over 65','Only in wealthy areas','They serve underserved communities.'),q('“Health in All Policies” means:','Considering health effects in decisions across all sectors','Only hospitals make health policy','Health care is free','Ignoring social determinants','Every sector affects health.'),q('According to KFF, the expanded Child Tax Credit in 2021 was associated with:','Reduced child poverty, especially for Black and Hispanic children','Increased uninsured rates','No change in poverty','Higher medical debt','Poverty rebounded after it expired.'),q('PEPFAR, launched in 2003, is a U.S. program focused on:','HIV/AIDS relief globally','Rural broadband','Kidney transplants','Medicare coverage','It is a major global health initiative.'),q('Medicaid expansion comparisons between states are an example of a:','Natural experiment','Randomized trial','Case report','Clinical guideline','Some states expanded and others did not.'),q('A strong policy evaluation for equity examines:','Whether gaps between groups narrowed, not only averages','Only total spending','Only the number of laws passed','Only hospital profits','Equity requires group comparisons.')],frq:{prompt:'Choose one health care policy and one policy outside health care. Explain how each could reduce health disparities, what evidence supports it, and how you would evaluate its effect on equity.',rubric:[['Health care policy explained',['medicaid','aca','health center','chip','coverage']],['Non-health policy explained',['tax credit','housing','income','paid sick','food']],['Evidence cited',['kff','14.5','8.0','child poverty','2021']],['Equity evaluation',['gap','by group','compare','before and after','monitor']]],model:'Medicaid expansion under the Affordable Care Act extends coverage to low-income adults, giving them a usual source of care and reducing cost barriers; KFF reports that in 2024 the uninsured rate was 14.5% in non-expansion states compared with 8.0% in expansion states. The temporarily expanded Child Tax Credit in 2021 raised family incomes and, according to KFF, was associated with reduced child poverty, especially among Black and Hispanic children, before poverty rebounded when it expired. To evaluate equity effects, I would compare outcomes before and after each policy and between places with and without it, stratify coverage, access and health outcomes by race, ethnicity, income and geography, and check whether gaps between groups narrowed, while monitoring for unintended effects.'},sources:[RULES,HP2030,HRSA_HC,KFF_101,WHO_EQ]};

export const W10={title:'Community-centered interventions and how to evaluate them',hook:'At two Indian Health Service clinics in the Navajo Nation, a telehealth team called patients with heart failure at home, helped them check their blood pressure and adjusted medications by phone. Within 30 days, far more patients were taking recommended heart failure medicines than with usual care. Interventions designed with and for communities can close gaps—when they are evaluated carefully.',objectives:['Describe community health workers, community-based interventions and culturally tailored care.','Summarize the NIMHD-highlighted Navajo Nation heart failure telehealth study.','Evaluate an intervention’s evidence and its effects on equity.','Synthesize course concepts for the essay tiebreaker.'],sections:[
['Community health workers and culturally tailored care',[
'**Community health workers (CHWs)** are trusted members of the communities they serve who help people navigate care, provide health education, connect families to social services and support chronic disease management. The CDC describes CHWs as an effective strategy for improving access and outcomes, especially for underserved populations. Related roles include promotoras in Hispanic communities and community health representatives in tribal communities. **Culturally tailored interventions** adapt content, language, setting and delivery to a community’s values and practices—for example, diabetes education that uses traditional foods or programs delivered through faith organizations. **Patient navigators** guide patients through complex care such as cancer treatment.',
match('Match the role or approach',[['Trusted community member who connects people to care','Community health worker'],['Spanish-speaking community health worker role','Promotora'],['Guides patients through complex treatment steps','Patient navigator'],['Adapting programs to a community’s language and values','Cultural tailoring'],['Research done in partnership with communities','Community-based participatory research']])]],
['A spotlight from NIMHD',[
'NIMHD highlighted a randomized clinical trial among 103 adults with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction receiving care at two Indian Health Service clinics in rural Navajo (Diné) Nation. The recommended treatment, guideline-directed medical therapy (GDMT), involves several medications whose doses are increased over time—usually requiring repeated clinic visits that can be hard to reach in rural areas. In the intervention, participants received a home blood pressure cuff and training, and a telehealth team discussed medications by phone, followed up after one week and called every one to two weeks to increase doses to the maximum tolerated level, with clinic blood work one to two weeks after starting new medicines. Usual-care patients were moved to telehealth in staggered groups.',
'At 30 days, 66.2% of telehealth patients had filled their recommended medications compared with 13.1% of usual-care patients, and the authors reported that the intervention increased uptake and reduced hospitalizations more than usual care. Because calls did not depend on specialist availability, the model addressed distance and workforce barriers directly. This is the study referenced in the HOSA sample question about a phone-based intervention with Navajo patients.',
bench('Is the claim supported by the NIMHD spotlight?',[{text:'NIMHD: at 30 days, 66.2% of telehealth patients had GDMT medications filled versus 13.1% of usual-care patients.',claim:'The phone-based intervention was associated with much higher uptake of recommended heart failure medications at 30 days.',supported:true,why:'The reported percentages show this difference in a randomized trial.'},{text:'NIMHD: the trial included 103 adults at two Indian Health Service clinics in rural Navajo Nation.',claim:'The study proves the intervention works equally well in every country.',supported:false,why:'Results apply to the population studied; other settings need their own evaluation.'}])]],
['Evaluating interventions for equity',[
'Evaluate an intervention by asking: Who designed it, and were community members involved? What outcome was measured, and was it meaningful to patients? What was the comparison—usual care, a waitlist, another program? Was the design randomized, before-and-after or observational? How large was the effect, and for how long? Did it narrow the gap between groups or improve averages while leaving gaps? What did it cost, and can it be sustained and scaled? Who might be left out—for example, people without phones or internet?',
order('Plan and evaluate a community intervention',['Partner with community members to identify priorities','Analyze causes using a framework such as NIMHD’s domains and levels','Co-design an intervention that fits local culture and resources','Choose outcome measures and a comparison group','Implement and collect data stratified by group','Share results with the community and adapt or scale the program'])]],
['Other community strategies',[
'Examples of promising strategies include **school-based health centers** that bring care to students, **mobile clinics** and **community paramedicine** that reach rural or homeless populations, **telehealth** with language and technology support, **faith-based health programs**, **food-as-medicine** programs such as produce prescriptions, and **medical-legal partnerships** that help patients resolve housing and benefits problems affecting health. Each targets specific social determinants. Combining clinical care with social support often produces larger effects than either alone.',
kase('Design a community intervention',{a:{text:'A county finds that uncontrolled high blood pressure is much more common in one neighborhood with limited transportation and many Spanish-speaking residents. What should the health department do first?',choices:[['Meet with community members and organizations to understand barriers and priorities','b','Correct. Community partnership improves design and trust.',true],['Mail English-only brochures to every household','b','This ignores language and community input.',false]]},b:{text:'Residents identify transportation, cost of medications and lack of Spanish-speaking clinicians as barriers. Which intervention fits best?',choices:[['Promotoras providing home blood pressure checks, medication support and links to a sliding-scale clinic with interpreters','c','Correct. It addresses language, access and cost together.',true],['A single citywide television advertisement','c','A broad message does not address the specific barriers.',false]]},c:{text:'The program measures blood pressure control by neighborhood and language group before and after launch, compares results with a similar neighborhood and shares findings at community meetings.',end:true}})]],
['Bringing the course together',[
'Across this course, you have defined health and equity, measured disparities with rates, examined disparities by race, income, geography, disability, gender, sexual orientation and language, explored how bias, research and tools affect treatment, and studied policies and community programs that reduce disparities. For the essay tiebreaker, organize answers around a clear thesis, cite specific facts from the official references—WHO, NIMHD, KFF, Our World in Data and Barr’s *Health Disparities in the US*—and explain causes at multiple levels before proposing solutions. Record figures exactly with their source and year, because test questions often ask for specific numbers from the referenced pages.',
flip('Course review',[['WHO definition of health','Complete physical, mental and social well-being, not merely absence of disease'],['NIMHD framework','Five domains × four levels of influence'],['Uninsured 2024 (KFF)','26.7 million nonelderly people, 9.8%'],['WHO PHC projection','+3.7 years life expectancy and 60 million lives saved by 2030 in LMICs'],['Unequal Treatment (2003)','Lower-quality care for minorities even with comparable insurance'],['Navajo telehealth trial','66.2% vs 13.1% filled heart failure medicines at 30 days']])]],
['Writing the essay tiebreaker',[
"The World Health & Disparities test includes an essay tiebreaker. A strong essay answers the prompt directly in a thesis sentence, then supports it with specific evidence from the official references, explains causes at more than one level, proposes realistic solutions and acknowledges a limitation. For example, an essay on infant mortality disparities could open with a thesis that the gap reflects structural and health care factors, cite KFF’s 2022 rates (10.9 per 1,000 for Black infants and 4.5 for White infants), explain contributors using the NIMHD framework, propose postpartum coverage extension and community health worker support, and note that evaluation should track whether the gap narrows.",
"Practice under time limits and use precise numbers only when you are confident of them; an approximate but clearly qualified figure (“more than twice as high”) is better than an invented precise one. Name your sources—WHO, NIMHD, KFF, Our World in Data or Barr—because the references are the basis of the test. Avoid language that blames individuals or stereotypes groups, and connect solutions to the causes you identified so that your argument is coherent from start to finish.",
{t:'table',caption:"Essay structure",head:["Part","What to include"],rows:[["Thesis","A one-sentence answer to the prompt"],["Evidence","Specific facts with source and year"],["Causes","Factors at individual, community and societal levels"],["Solutions","Policies and programs linked to those causes"],["Limitation","What remains uncertain or how to evaluate progress"]]}]]
],takeaways:['CHWs, promotoras, navigators and culturally tailored programs build trust and access.','In the NIMHD-highlighted Navajo Nation trial, phone-based heart failure care raised medication uptake to 66.2% vs 13.1% at 30 days.','Evaluate interventions with community partnership, comparison groups, stratified outcomes and sustainability.'],terms:[['Community health worker','Trusted community member who links people to care and services.'],['Guideline-directed medical therapy','Recommended medication regimen for heart failure.'],['Medical-legal partnership','Collaboration that addresses legal needs affecting health.']],mcqs:[q('What medical condition was addressed by the phone-based intervention with Navajo patients highlighted by NIMHD?','Heart failure','Diabetes','Parkinson’s','ALS','The trial addressed heart failure with reduced ejection fraction.'),q('In the NIMHD-highlighted trial, what share of telehealth patients had filled recommended medications at 30 days?','66.2%','13.1%','100%','25%','Usual care was 13.1%.'),q('Where did the Navajo Nation telehealth trial take place?','Two Indian Health Service clinics in rural Navajo Nation','A large urban hospital','A university laboratory','A pharmacy chain','Distance barriers shaped the design.'),q('Community health workers are best described as:','Trusted community members who connect people to care and services','Hospital executives','Insurance agents','Researchers only','CHWs bridge communities and systems.'),q('Cultural tailoring means:','Adapting interventions to a community’s language, values and practices','Using one message for everyone','Removing community input','Translating only the title','Fit improves effectiveness.'),q('Which is a key question when evaluating an intervention for equity?','Did it narrow the gap between groups?','Did it use a logo?','Was it expensive?','Was it on television?','Equity requires examining gaps.'),q('A medical-legal partnership helps patients with:','Legal problems such as housing or benefits that affect health','Surgery scheduling','Insurance billing codes only','Prescription refills only','It addresses social determinants.'),q('What should a health department do first when designing a community intervention?','Partner with community members to understand barriers and priorities','Launch a citywide ad','Mail brochures in one language','Close the local clinic','Partnership guides design.')],frq:{prompt:'Using the NIMHD-highlighted Navajo Nation heart failure trial as an example, explain how a community-centered intervention can reduce a disparity, and describe how you would evaluate a similar program in a different community.',rubric:[['Describe the Navajo trial accurately',['heart failure','navajo','indian health service','telehealth','phone','66.2','13.1']],['Explain barriers addressed',['distance','rural','specialist','visit','access']],['Evaluation design',['comparison','randomiz','stratif','outcome','gap']],['Community partnership and sustainability',['community','partner','sustain','scale','tailor']]],model:'NIMHD highlighted a randomized trial among 103 adults with heart failure at two Indian Health Service clinics in rural Navajo Nation, where reaching clinics for repeated medication adjustments is difficult. A telehealth team provided home blood pressure cuffs and called patients every one to two weeks to adjust guideline-directed medications, so care no longer depended on travel or specialist availability. At 30 days, 66.2% of telehealth patients had filled recommended medications versus 13.1% with usual care, and hospitalizations were reduced. To evaluate a similar program elsewhere, I would partner with community members to tailor it, choose meaningful outcomes, use a comparison group or staggered rollout, stratify results by group to see whether gaps narrow, check who is left out such as people without phones, and assess cost and sustainability before scaling.'},sources:[RULES,NIMHD_NAVAJO,CDC_CHW,NIMHD]};
